Windows: on a software adapter (WARP: a hosted Windows runner, a GPU-less VM, remote desktop),
a process that builds engine after engine still ends partway through (tracked separately, row
WARP-ACCUMULATION).

On a software adapter only, every queue submit in the compositor, the renderer and the canvas
now waits for that submission (Maintain::WaitForSubmissionIndex) before the call returns. The
compositor sets the switch from its adapter's device type (Cpu); on a hardware GPU the switch is
off and nothing changes.

Pin (local, forced WARP, the layout corpus in one process): without the wait it ends partway
through; with it, 23 of 23 pages and ok in three runs out of three (about 30% slower on WARP).
Source pin: rustkit-compositor tests::every_submit_settles_on_a_software_adapter (red on a mutant
that drops one wait).

Remove when the wgpu upgrade row lands and the corpus runs in one process on WARP without it,
or move it upstream if macOS wants the guard too.

